### Implemented enclosure and meaning of a rejection

"'`models.py` imports the fixed NPZ leaf bits as float64 without training, generates the automaton, and proposes
centres. `verified.py` independently evaluates the maps and their derivatives with 768-bit Arb balls. The target
is the **real-arithmetic interpretation** of the fixed weight bits and specified dyadic program constants, with
mathematical sqrt/exp/tanh. This is not a claim about every floating-point implementation of the recurrence.

For a source ellipsoid `c + B z`, `||z|| <= r`, the verifier follows the centre'"'s actual trajectory through every
attention/MLP tick and closure. It encloses each sublayer Jacobian "'`D`, the full preceding product `L`, and the
product `K` since the current event began. Let `e` be the accumulated error at the start of the event and `a` the
new remainder inside that event. Each sublayer uses

```
delta <= ||L B|| r + ||K|| e + a
new_a <= ||D|| a + H(delta) delta''^2 / 2.
```

At the event boundary, `new_e <= ||J_event|| e + new_a`. Thus earlier remainders retain the event Jacobian'"'s
cancellation instead of being multiplied by every intervening sublayer norm. No trajectory is reset to a target
centre. The resulting sufficient block condition is

"'```
b + gamma r_source + ||B_target''^-1|| e(r_source) <= r_target,
b >= ||B_target''^-1 (F_word(c_source) - c_target)||,
gamma >= ||B_target''^-1 J_word(c_source) B_source||.
```

For normalisation, embed layer norm in `(C h, sqrt(d epsilon))`. The radial normalisation'"'s second directional
derivative is at most "'`(2/sqrt(3)) sqrt(d) / n_min''^2`; projecting the augmented output and centering cannot increase
this bound. One way to check the constant is to write the squared norm for a unit direction as
`(1-t''^2)(1+3t''^2) <= 4/3`, where `t` is its radial component. The code uses the actual dyadic closure scale in the
closure bound. The layer-norm first-derivative bound is `sqrt(d)/n_min`.

The attention constants bound the covariance and second central moment of the fixed key/value softmax, retaining
all tokens (masked Lean scores are the actual constant -1e9, not silently deleted). For the tanh GELU, an exhaustive
**interval cover**, not point sampling, encloses the first two derivatives on [-16,16]. Polynomial-times-exponential
tail bounds cover both infinite tails. The verified global bounds used are `|GELU'"'| <= 1.2"'` and `|GELU'"''| <= 0.9"'`.
The MLP and normalisation bounds combine by the chain rule. These are conservative global activation bounds;
they do not exploit neuron-specific activation intervals or directional Hessian cancellation.

Every spectral-norm proposal from numerical SVD is checked in Arb. For `A = U diag(s) V + E`, the accepted bound is
`||E||_F + s_max sqrt(1+||U''^T U-I||_F) sqrt(1+||V V''^T-I||_F)`. Inverses, residuals, functions, and comparisons are
also enclosed. Frobenius squares explicitly intersect with the nonnegative reals; all non-finite enclosures abort.
Floating-point shape and centre fitting only select exact dyadic candidate matrices/vectors; it is not proof evidence.

Readout caps use the layer-norm chord bound and `||B z|| <= ||B|| r`, with every competing token/class checked.
They are **sufficient caps for this verifier**, not the exact maximal ellipsoid inside each decoding cell. Starting
all radii at zero and applying the monotone sufficient containment right-hand sides gives necessary lower bounds
on any solution of these particular inequalities. Crossing a readout cap proves that this sufficient system is
infeasible. The replay records use lower interval endpoints for those infeasibility steps, and bracket the maximal
per-edge admitted source radius using upper endpoints to accept and lower endpoints to reject. A rejected sufficient
system does not prove that the underlying nonlinear set inclusion is false.

The initial-state clause and intermediate readout clauses are deliberately omitted only in the **rejection phase**.
Its infeasibility therefore also rules out direct entry and exact-prefix entry under the same selected centres,
shapes, caps, and containment bounds. A subset survivor triggers an error requiring universal expansion; it cannot
be silently reported as either PASS or a completed KILL.
